{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Convex.Jensen","lean:module:Mathlib.RepresentationTheory.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.optima_coincide","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.optima_coincide","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L131-L165","doc":"**The offset vanishes — two invariant objectives share their optimum.**\n\nThis is the statement the angle-structure probe needs. Let `f₁` and `f₂` be any\ntwo concave objectives on the same symmetric convex feasible set, each invariant\nunder the symmetry and each with a unique maximiser. Then both maximisers equal\nthe symmetric point `p`, hence equal each other: **the offset between the two\noptima is zero, whatever the two objectives are.**\n\nApplied to the angle-structure polytope, `f₁` is the Casson-Rivin minimum-angle\nobjective and `f₂` the volume functional; both are concave and invariant under\nthe triangulation's symmetry, so no offset between `x_LP` and `x_geom` can exist\n— which is what the probe measured as `2.4·10⁻¹⁶` and `5.0·10⁻¹⁶`.\n\nThe contrapositive is the useful half going forward: an offset that carries\ninformation requires an objective that is **not** invariant. The confinement\nSDP's objective is the braid's Markov-trace data, which breaks the symmetry —\nso that is where a nonzero offset can live. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.symmetric_point_optimal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.symmetric_point_optimal","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L111-L129","doc":"**The symmetric point is optimal when it is unique.**\n\nIf `p` is the only symmetric feasible point, then no feasible point beats it:\nevery `x ∈ K` averages into a symmetric feasible point, which must be `p`, and\naveraging did not decrease `f`.\n\nThis is the statement behind the vanishing offset. Two different concave\ninvariant objectives on the same symmetric convex feasible set are both\nmaximised at `p`, so their optima coincide and the offset between them is `0` —\nno matter what the objectives are. Consequently an informative offset requires\nan objective that is **not** invariant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.exists_symmetric_maximiser","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.exists_symmetric_maximiser","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L102-L109","doc":"**A symmetric maximiser always exists.** Given any feasible `x`, the orbit\naverage is feasible, symmetric, and at least as good. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.le_apply_orbitAvg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.le_apply_orbitAvg","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L87-L100","doc":"**Averaging never hurts a concave invariant objective** (Jensen). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.orbitAvg_fixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.orbitAvg_fixed","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L78-L85","doc":"**The orbit average is symmetric.** Acting by `h` permutes the sum, because\nleft translation `g ↦ h * g` is a bijection of `G`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.orbitAvg_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.orbitAvg_mem","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L70-L76","doc":"**The orbit average stays feasible.** Convexity of `K` plus stability under\nthe action. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.orbitAvg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum.orbitAvg","kind":"def","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/SymmetricOptimum.lean#L54-L57","doc":"The orbit average of `x` under a representation `ρ`: the barycentre of the\n`G`-orbit, with equal weights. ","signatureChunk":0}]}